What was the Taconic orogeny and how did it affect the geology of the region between New York and Quebec, Canada?
The Taconic orogeny was a mountain building period that ended 440 million years ago, affecting most of modern-day New England and eastern Canada. It formed a great mountain chain from eastern Canada down through the Piedmont of the East coast of the United States. The orogeny resulted in the formation of a great mountain range throughout New England and eastern Canada, and the newly expanded continental margin gradually stabilized. 

The Taconic orogeny led to the formation of metamorphic rocks such as amphibolite, mica schist, and granites in the region between New York and Quebec, Canada. These rocks formed a belt running down the Gaspe Peninsula to Sherbrooke, and also formed around Asbestos, Becancour, and Quebec City. The orogeny also led to the deposition of sediments throughout the present-day Appalachians and midcontinental North America.
